# Mental State Examination

- **Role of the mental state examination:**
  - Assessment of the S/S and behaviour displayed during the interview
  - Elicit or record symptoms not explicitly covered in the interview
- **Components of the mental state examination:**
  - Appearance and behaviour
  - Speech
  - Thoughts
  - Mood and Affect
  - Perceptions
  - Cognitive function
  - Insight
- **Appearance and behaviour** - provisional diagnosis can be made within minutes of meeting the patient by heavily relying on this information:
  - **General appearance** - overall sense of mannerism in the waiting room:
    - <u>Depression</u> - often sitting quietly
    - <u>Anxiety</u> - pacing around the room
    - <u>Mania</u> - may be laughing to themselves
  - **Grooming and attire:**
    - <u>Unkept appearnace</u> - reflects self-neglect
    - <u>Bright and incongrous attire</u> - manic episode
    - <u>Oddity of atire</u> (e.g. raincoat in sunny day) - can be first evidence of a disorder of thought
  - **Body build** - e.g. weight loss or recent weight gain:
    - <u>Weight loss</u> - alert of possibility of depressive disorder, anorexia nervosa or physical illness
    - <u>Weight gain</u> - may be an eating disorder, but may also be seen in depressive disorders less commonly
  - **Facial appearance and emotional expression:**
    - <u>Depressed facies</u> - corner of mouth turned down, vertical furrows on the brow
    - <u>Anxiety</u> - Omega sign (horizontal creases on forehead, widened palpebral fissures, dilated pupils)
    - <u>Elated appearance</u> - facial expression may reflect elation, irritability etc.
    - <u>Fixed 'wooden' expression</u> - extra-pyramidal manifestations of antipsychotics (drug-induced parkisonism)
    - <u>Thyrotoxic facies</u> - lid retraction, exophthalmos etc.
  - **Posture and psychomotor activity:**
    - <u>Stooped posture</u> - hunched shoulder, with head and gaze included downwards classically in a depressed patient
    - <u>Agitation</u> - e.g. picking fingernails, tremulous and restless seen in patients with anxious patients or agitated depression
    - <u>Tardive dyskinesia</u> - hyperkinetic disorder due to the effects of dopamine receptor blockade
    - <u>Catatonia</u> - psychomotor S/S seen mainly in catatonic disorders (e.g. depression, schizophrenia)
  - **Social behaviour** - interpreted by the interactions w/ the interviewer, influenced by 1) personality, 2) attitude towards interview, and 3) prevailing mood or pre-occupied thoughts
- **Speech** - covers 1) rate, 2) quantity, 3) volume, and 4) flow of speech, as opposed to the contents of speech:
  - <u>Difficulties w/ speaking</u> - consider possibility of dysphasia or dysarthria
  - <u>Rate and quantity</u> - pressure of speech in mania, or slow, sparse, and monotomous (reflecting psychomotor retardation) in depression
  - <u>Choice of words</u> - e.g. clang associations, neologism
  - <u>Flow of speech</u> - assess stream and formal of thoughts
- **Thoughts:**
  - <u>Predominant content of thoughts</u> - assessed based on response to questions, and whether drive conversation to a particular topic, further classified based on themes (e.g. persecution/ reference, related to eating and diet, negative cognition)
  - <u>Phenomenology of thought</u> - abnormalities include 1) Obsessions and compulsions, and 2) delusions:
    - **Obsessions** - described as 1) persistent and intrusive, 2) known to arise from one's mind, 3) difficult to control w/ thoughts of avoidance, and 4) egodystonic (e.g. ashamed for violence etc), which may be accompanied w/ compulsive acts (physical or mental)
    - **Delusions** - unable to be derived directly, usually elicited from other people or by events in the history, and characterise first-rank symptoms (thought interference, delusional perception, passivity)
  - <u>Formal thought disorder</u> - tangentiality, word salad etc.
- **Mood and affect** - record pervasive mood, fluctuations of mood, morbid emotional expressions, and related phenomenon:
  - **Approach to assessment of mood:**
    - Observations of appearance, behaviour and thoughts
    - Direct questioning or subjective rating of mood (e.g. what is your mood like?)
    - Indirect questioning (see below)
  - **Pervasive mood** - depression, euthymia, or mania:
    - **Depression:**
      - <u>Assessment of low mood</u> - e.g. any feeling of about to cry? (N.B. most will not admit actual tearfulness)
      - <u>Assessment of pessimistic thoughts</u> - e.g. what do you think will happen to you in the future? Have you been blaming yourself for anything?
      - <u>Assessment of anhedonia</u> - e.g. have you found yourself unable to enjoy your hobbies?
    - **Mania:**
      - <u>Assessment of elated mood</u> - e.g. do you find yourself in unusually good spirits?
      - <u>Assessment of irritability</u> - e.g. have you been more irritable lately?
  - **Fluctuating and incongruous mood** - notice with the variations in mood in relations w/ the topics being discussed:
    - <u>Range of affect</u> - emotional lability vs affect flattening
    - <u>Congruence with mood</u> - whether emotional expression is congruent or not congruent w/ prevailing mood
  - **Anxiety** - addressing physical Sx and cognitive responses to anxiety:
    - <u>Physical Sx</u> - general questioning (e.g. have you noticed any changes in body when you feel upset) followed by specific questions
    - <u>Anxious cognition</u> - ask about what goes on in the mind when feeling anxious
  - **Suicidal ideation** - from more open questions to narrower questions:
    - Have you ever thought that life is not worth living?
    - Have you ever wished that you could die?
    - Have you ever considered that you might end your life?
    - Have you ever considered any way in which you might end your life?
  - **Depersonalisation and rerealisation** - often attributed to severe depression, or as a separate disorder
- **Perception** - hallucination tactfully elicited
- **Cognitive function:**
  - <u>Orientation</u> - awareness of time, place, and person specifically:
    - Disorientation of time may be understandable
    - Isolated disorientation of own's identity occurs only in severe dementia, dissociative disorders, or malingering
  - <u>Attention and concentration</u> - can be inferred from ability to pay attention and concentrate on the interview:
    - Formal testing includes serial sevens, but note that it may be due to poor arithmic skills
    - State the months or year in reverse order to bypass arithmic limitations
  - <u>Memory</u> - compare patient's acount of past events w/ informant to identify gaps, consistencies:
    - Identify evidence of confabulation
    - Evaluate short-term memory can be assessed through standardised test
    - Memory for recent events can be assessed by asking about news items from the last few days
    - Remote memory assessed by asking the patient to recall personal events or well-known items of news from former years
- **Insight:**
  - Recognise experience as abnormal
  - Recognise experience to be ascribed to a mental illness
  - Recgonises the need for treatment
  - Recognises the use of treatment correlating to illness/ experience
